Cellulose is the most resource-rich biopolymer on earth that can be extracted from many natural plants, algae, and microorganisms. Benefiting from its renewability, degradability, and unique physicochemical properties, it has been widely employed in various applications such as paper, packaging, and water treatment [5]. Cellulose can also be further deconstructed to obtain nanostructure through chemical or physical treatments, referred as nanocellulose. Typically, nanocellulose is divided into three types: (i) Cellulose nanocrystals (CNs) with rod-like shape are generally prepared by acid hydrolysis; (ii) cellulose nanofibers (CNF) with web-like entangled morphologies are ubiquitously obtained by mechanical disintegration; and (iii) bacterial cellulose (BC) with three-dimensional (3D) interconnected networks is extracted from the fermentation of microorganisms [6–8]. Nanocellulose possesses better mechanical properties owing to its high aspect ratio and high Young's modulus, along with the advantage of large specific surface area, hydrophilicity, transparency, low thermal expansion coefficient, and flexible surface chemistry, making it a strong candidate for fabricating advanced materials in various fields [9–11]. Numerous efforts have been devoted to combining nanocellulose as matrices with conductive materials such as carbon materials, conductive polymers, and metal/metal oxide nanoparticles to obtain advanced conductive composites [12–15]. Remarkably, nanocellulose can also be converted into conductive carbonaceous materials with
hierarchical porous structures by a simple carbonization process [16]. As a consequence, the low-cost and eco-friendly nanocellulose is quite suitable for green and sustainable electronic and energy applications.
As an increasingly popular material, many nanocellulose-based reviews for energy-related devices have been published in recent years, including supercapacitors, batteries, energy harvesting devices, and sensors [17–20]. However, there are still few reviews that have concurrently discussed energy storage/harvesting devices and sensors based on nanocellulose and its derivatives. There is no doubt that energy devices and sensors are two inseparable parts of the future intelligent world. And nanocellulose-based functional materials are also undergoing rapid development in the fields of energy and sensing. Therefore, the purpose of this review is to comprehensively summarize and analyze the recent progress of nanocellulose-based advanced composites in energy-related areas of SCs, lithium-ion batteries (LIBs), solar cells, nanogenerators, and sensors. We firstly introduce the preparation approaches for processing nanocellulose into advanced conductive materials, including the types of conductive materials and corresponding fabrication methodologies. After that, the applications of nanocellulose-based functional materials in SCs, LIBs, solar cells, triboelectric nanogenerators (TENGs), moisture-enabled electric generators (MEEGs), and sensors are comprehensively discussed. Finally, future perspectives for the development of nanocellulose-based functional materials in energy-related fields are briefly presented. Overall, this review is expected to inspire broader research interest in nanocellulose-based functional materials and play a guiding role in the creation and development of next-generation energy and sensing devices.
## 2. Conductive nanocellulose composites
Conductive materials are considered to be objects capable of transporting and conducting electrical currents. Nanocellulose with a unique one-dimensional (1D) structure and chemical diversity can provide excellent performance enhancements that are difficult to achieve with traditional synthetic materials, making it widely used in combination with conductive materials to prepare novel composites. Many types of conductive materials including carbon, conductive polymer, metal/metal oxide nanoparticles, metal-organic frameworks (MOFs), and covalent organic frameworks (COFs) have been combined with nanocellulose, the obtained conductive composites usually contain the advantages of both components. More details are described in the following sections.
## 2.1 Nanocellulose-carbon composites
Carbon materials have received wide attention due to their higher conductivity than conductive polymer, as well as high tensile strength. However, nanocarbon is hard to be processed into materials or independent substrates due to aggregation and difficulties in processing. Thus, nanocellulose with amphiphilic character, high aspect ratio, and large surface area can act as ideal supporting materials to combine nanocarbon. Carbon nanotubes (CNTs) and graphene are most commonly used in the fabrication of nanocellulose/carbon composites. CNTs have excellent physical properties (mechanical, electrical, optical, and thermal), which can be classified into two main types: single-walled carbon nanotubes (SWCNTs) and multi-walled carbon nanotubes (MWCNTs). The high affinity between CNTs and nanocellulose makes them natural partners in composites materials. Graphene is a two-dimensional (2D) structure with an atomic thickness that provided versatile electrochemical properties and exceptional mechanical properties including Young's modulus of 1 TPa, ultimate strength of \(130\,\mathrm{GPa}\), and thermal conductivity of around \(5,000\,\mathrm{W\cdot m^{-1}\cdot K^{-1}}\). The obtained nanocellulose-graphene composites are highly porous, with excellent shape retention. CNTs and graphene as desirable conductive materials can be integrated with nanocellulose through coating or blending.
ultimate strength of \(130\,\mathrm{GPa}\) , and thermal conductivity of around \(5,000\,\mathrm{W\cdot m^{-1}\cdot K^{-1}}\) . The obtained nanocellulose-graphene composites are highly porous, with excellent shape retention. CNTs and graphene as desirable conductive materials can be integrated with nanocellulose through coating or blending.
The coating technique is an effective method for preparing nanocellulose-carbon composites, where nanocellulose is typically used as the substrate for carbon materials. Hu et al. prepared conductive CNF nanopaper by Meyer rod coating method, where CNT was printed on nanopaper substrate [21]. The nanopaper showed highly transparent and lower resistance \((200\ \Omega{\cdot}\mathrm{sq}^{-1})\). Lea et al. fabricated MWCNT/cellulose papers via the facile dip-coating process, which achieved an apparent electrical conductivity in the in-plane direction of \(1.11\ \mathrm{S{\cdot}c m^{-1}}\) [14]. However, the improvement of conductivity is a problem, which cannot be achieved by simply increasing the coating layer thickness or amount, due to the weak bonding between carbon materials in the coating layer which can cause the layer to fall off. By contrast, in the blending method, more carbon materials can be incorporated into nanocellulose substrate by physically entrapping, resulting in an enhancement of conductivity. Hamedi et al. [22] reported the semitransparent conductive SWCNTs/CNF films, which achieved a high conductivity of \(200\ \mathrm{S{\cdot}c m^{-1}}\). Other carbon materials such as MWCNTs [23], graphene oxide (GO) [24], and its derivative reduced graphene oxide (rGO) [25] were also successfully employed to prepare conductive composites. Meanwhile, the conductive performance of nanocellulose-carbon composites can be controlled by the loading amount of carbon materials [23]. Dang et al. [26] reported the CNF/rGO composite papers, where the electrical conductivity was increased from \(7.3\times10^{-4}\ \mathrm{S{\cdot}c m^{-1}}\) with the addition of \(1\ \mathrm{wt.\%}\) rGO to \(0.154\ \mathrm{S{\cdot}c m^{-1}}\) with the addition of \(10\ \mathrm{wt.\%}\) rGO.
## 2.2 Nanocellulose-conductive polymers composites
Conductive polymers were developed in the 1970s and employed in battery industries in the 1980s as alternatives to metallic materials due to lightweight, low-cost, as well as the conjugated \(\pi\)-bonds in the polymer structure that provide unique electrical, electrochemical, and optical properties. The conductive polymers, such as polyaniline (PANI), polypyrrole (PPy), polyacetylene (PA), polythiophene (PT), poly[3,4-(ethylenedioxy)thiophene] (PEDOT), and polyphenylene, usually derive from a few basic polymer structures. And they have been widely used in various fields including electrochemical capacitors, batteries, solar cells, sensors, electrical conductors, and electromagnetic shielding materials [19]. However, the strong interaction between chains leads to poor solubility and weak mechanical performance, limiting the further development of conductive polymers. To that end, nanocellulose has been investigated to combine with conductive polymers (particularly PPy or PANI) to obtain conductive nanocomposites with excellent mechanical properties, good flexibility, and high electrical conductivity. And in situ polymerization was most commonly used to introduce conductive polymers into the nanocellulose matrix.
Generally, the in situ polymerization process is that the nanocellulose is well dispersed in a concoction of protonic acid and polar solvent to form a suspension. The monomers and oxidant agents such as ammonium persulfate are then added to initiate the polymerization on the surface of the nanocellulose to obtain a nanocomposite of nanocellulose-conductive polymer. Flandin et al. [27] firstly studied the polymerization of PPy on CNF to prepare nanocomposite, which showed great potential in sensor application. After that, many studies have focused on the influence factors on the conductivity of nanocomposites including the type of nanocellulose [28], polymerization conditions [29],
conductive particle distribution [30], and morphology structure [31]. It is found that the type of nanocellulose and conductive particle distribution are major factors affecting the conductivity of the nanocellulose/conductive polymer composites. Besides, the hydrogen bond interactions between –OH groups of nanocellulose and functional groups of conductive polymer (such as –NH groups of Py monomer or PANI) [32, 33] are essential to prepare a stable nanocomposite, as well as to improve the mechanical properties. The surface functionalized modification of nanocellulose can effectively enhance the interaction between nanocellulose and conductive polymers, resulting in excellent conductive composites with better mechanical performance. Jradi et al. [34] prepared TEMPO-oxidized CNFs/PPy composite films, where carboxylate moieties were introduced on CNFs surface by TEMPO oxidation treatment. The presence of carboxylate and hydroxyl moieties significantly improved the adsorption process of positively charged PPy backbone during polymerization. The obtained films exhibited good mechanical properties without sacrificing the high electrical conductivity.
## 2.3 Nanocellulose-metal nanoparticle composites
Metal nanoparticles have ultra-high inherent conductivity \((\sim 105\ \mathrm{S\cdot cm^{-1}})\) and excellent magnetic effect, but the drawbacks such as unstable thermodynamics, high density, and high price limit their application range. Nanocellulose has a good stabilizing effect, which can effectively facilitate the nucleation of metal nanoparticles and prevent their agglomeration. Therefore, the combination with nanocellulose to prepare conductive composite is beneficial to enriching the uses. Precious metals such as silver and gold have been extensively studied, which are usually embedded into the nanocellulose matrix or directly coated on the nanocellulose surface. Hsieh et al. [35] prepared highly conductive circuits on cellulose nanopaper by a gold sputtering technique of printing with silver nanoparticles (AgNPs) inks, which successfully illuminated light-emitting diode (LED). Song et al. [36] fabricated ultrathin, highly transparent, and outstanding conductive nanopaper by the pressured extrusion paper-making technique, where silver nanowires (AgNWs) were embedded into nanofibrillated cellulose (NFC) networks using hydroxypropylmethyl cellulose as a cross-linker. The obtained nanopaper showed a high optical transmittance of \(86.41\%\) at \(550\ \mathrm{nm}\) and sheet resistance of \(1.90\ \mathrm{ohm\cdot sq^{-1}}\) (conductivity of \(\sim\,500\ \mathrm{S\cdot cm^{-1}}\)). Other techniques including drop coating [12], screen printing [37], and vacuum-assisted microcontact printing [38] were also used to embed AgNWs into the nanocellulose. Besides, metal nanoparticles such as copper [39], tin [21], ruthenium [40], and bismuth [41] were also introduced into the nanocellulose matrix to prepare conductive composites. In particular, copper is highly attractive to be used as a substitute, due to the low cost and relatively large availability. Yang et al. [42] prepared highly conductive, pliable, and foldable Cu/cellulose paper using a simple wet-processing method. The produced paper exhibited superior conductivity with a sheet resistance as low as 0.01 ohm·sq\(^{-1}\), which has promising application potential in flexible electronics.
## 2.4 Nanocellulose-metal oxide nanoparticle composites
Metal oxides can undergo fast and reversible Faradaic reactions that contribute to high energy density. However, they are generally restricted by poor conductivity and the reactions are confined to particles surfaces. Therefore, it is crucial to combine metal oxides with nanocellulose to construct composites with good electrochemical performance. So far, a diverse set of metal oxides such as \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) [43], \(\mathrm{ZnO}\) [44], \(\mathrm{CuO}\) [45], \(\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}\) [46], \(\mathrm{SnO}_{2}\) [47], \(\mathrm{MnO}_{2}\) [15], and \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}\) [48] have been explored to combine with
nanocellulose. The most common methods to prepare nanocellulose-metal oxide composites include sol–gel, atomic layer deposition (ALD), in situ synthesis, and layer-by-layer deposition. For example, Gutierrez et al. [49] reported \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}/\mathrm{BC}\) hybrid fibers in which BC fibers as a hydrophilic substrate for \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) nanoparticles were synthesized via sol-gel. And the \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) nanoparticles were located on BC fibers surface due to the hydrogen bonding interactions. Li et al. [50] synthesized high infrared blocking \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}/\) cellulose films using ALD. The heat insulator property of films was efficiently enhanced by adjusting ALD parameters. Chen et al. [15] fabricated flower-like \(\mathrm{MnO}_{2}\) CNC composites via a facile one-step in situ synthesis. The \(\mathrm{MnO}_{2}\) nanoparticles were uniformly distributed on CNC, resulting in an excellent electrochemical performance of the \(\mathrm{MnO}_{2}\) -CNC composite. Xiao et al. [48] successfully prepared nanocellulose/porous \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}\) polyhedron hybrid films by the layer-by-layer assembly. The obtained hybrid films as a free-standing electrode exhibited high capacitance, good flexibility, and foldability.
## 2.5 Nanocellulose-MOFs composites
MOFs are highly porous materials linked by metal ions and organic ligands via coordination bonds, which have excellent chemical stability, good pore accessibility, high surface area (up to \(7,000\ \mathrm{m^{2}\cdot g^{-1}}\)), and versatile functionalities [51]. But the stubborn powdered crystalline state and inherent low electrical conductivity of MOFs severely hinder their practical application [52]. Thus, it is of great significance to prepare MOFs composites with high conductivity, high processability, mechanical durability, and excellent flexibility. Nanocellulose with the hierarchical structure, low density, and mechanical flexibility has been extensively used as a desirable substrate to prepare advanced MOFs composites. Ex-situ growth is a common method to fabricate the nanocellulose-MOFs composites, where the synthesized MOFs are usually loaded into the nanocellulose through sol-gel casting or electrospinning process. It is worth noting that the affinity and compatibility of composites can be improved after the surface modification of nanocellulose [53]. In situ growth is another typical approach to obtain nanocellulose-MOFs composites, where MOFs are synthesized inside or on the nanocellulose surface. The advantages of in situ growth are that MOFs have controllable crystal size, uniform distribution, and sufficient affinity. Zhou et al. [54] reported the preparation of CNF@c-MOFs hybrid nanofibers, where MOFs nanolayers were grown on CNF substrates by interfacial synthesis. The CNF@c-MOFs hybrid nanofibers were further assembled into freestanding nanopaper, which exhibited high conductivity up to \(100\ \mathrm{S\cdot cm^{-1}}\), strong mechanical strength, and good flexibility that can be folded into complex origami structures.
## 2.6 Nanocellulose-COFs composites
COFs are novel crystalline porous polymers with pre-designable pore structures, highly tunable functionality, large surface area, and exceptional chemical stability, which are constructed by strong covalent bonds from reactions of light elements including boron, carbon, nitrogen, oxygen, and silicon [55, 56]. However, the practical use of COFs remains challenging due to their intrinsic drawbacks such as poor processing ability, insolubility, poor dispersion, and weak interlamellar interaction. To overcome these problems, nanocellulose has been investigated to integrate with COFs to obtain COFs-based composites with additional properties and functionalities. The surface modification of nanocellulose can improve its compatibility with COFs [57]. Yang et al. [58] prepared Schiff-base-type COF TpTGC@CNF membranes through a mixed-dimensional assembly strategy. The
sheltering effect of TEMPO-oxidized CNFs endowed \(\mathrm{TpTGCl}@\mathrm{CNF}\) membranes with precise molecular sieving ability. Meanwhile, the multiple interactions such as electrostatic and hydrogen bond interactions between COFs and CNFs significantly improved the stability of membranes. Kong et al. [59] fabricated flexible \(\mathrm{c-CNT@COF/CNT/CNF}\) nanopaper by a bottom-up interweaving strategy. The CNF was used as the substrate to knit and reinforce the \(\mathrm{c-CNT@COF}\) forming a uniform cross-linked network, and the nanopaper exhibited outstanding flexibility and foldability.
## 3 Applications of nanocellulose-based materials
## 3.1 Energy storage devices
## 3.1.1 Supercapacitors
The increasingly serious climate change and fossil fuel depletion problem urgently requests alternative energy sources to meet the needs of development. And the research of energy storage devices has become the priority to obtain renewable energy from the sun, wind, and other renewable sources. SCs have drawn great attention due to their high power density, fast charge–discharge rate, and excellent cycling stability, which bridge the gap between conventional capacitors and rechargeable batteries [60, 61]. SCs can be divided into three types: electrochemical double-layer capacitors (EDLCs), pseudocapacitors, and hybrid capacitors, according to the charge storage mechanism. Among them, EDLCs are the majority of the currently available commercial electrochemical capacitors owing to the maturity of technical. In EDLCs, capacitance is stored through the electrostatic adsorption of ions at the electrode/electrolyte interface, thus the capacitive performance is strongly dependent on the available surface area and conductivity of the electrode materials [62]. In contrast, the charge storage mechanism of pseudocapacitors is a Faradaic process in which a fast and reversible redox reaction occurs on the electrode surface, thus the capacitance mainly depends on the charge generation rate and storage ability of electrodes [63]. Compared with EDLCs which have higher power density and relatively long cycle lives, pseudocapacitors show higher energy density [64]. The hybrid capacitors consist of a carbon-based electrode and battery-type Faradaic electrode, which combine
charging/discharging mechanisms from both EDLCs and pseudocapacitors, offering the advantages of both SCs (high power density and long life span) and batteries (high energy density) [65, 66]. Nanocellulose has been extensively researched in energy storage devices, due to its good mechanical properties, high aspect ratio, high surface area, high porosity, and the ability to combine with other conductive materials [67, 68]. Although pure nanocellulose is an inherently insulating material, different forms of nanocellulose have been applied in SCs with high performance. According to the roles played by nanocellulose in SCs, it can be mainly divided into three types: (i) electrode materials which integrated with other conductive materials or converted to carbon materials through pyrolytic processes; (ii) separator materials between two SCs electrodes; (iii) electrolyte materials that facilitate the electrolyte ions adsorption. The electrochemical performance of nanocellulose-based supercapacitors has been compared and summarized in Table 1.
For SCs, the major challenge is insufficient energy density that cannot meet the increasing demand (commonly, EDLCs \(<\ 10\ \mathrm{\Wh{\cdot}k g^{-1}}\) and pseudocapacitors \(<\,50\ \mathrm{\Wh{\cdot}k g^{-1}}\) ) [69]. The effective way to increase the energy density is by improving capacitance and voltage window. It can be achieved by the development of carbon materials with a larger specific surface area and high packing density [70]. Therefore, there has been a growing interest in electrically conductive porous electrodes, as it provides more active sites for the adsorption of electrolyte ions. It can also improve energy density by developing pseudocapacitive materials with excellent specific capacitance. Nanocellulose with highly porous structures provides an ideal substrate for loading active materials, and active materials could be integrated with nanocellulose using different methods such as extrusion [71], vacuum filtration [72], and freeze-drying [73], to form various structures like 1D fibers, 2D papers/films, and 3D aerogels/hydrogels. Owing to the high aspect ratio and mechanical strength, nanocellulose-based electrodes show stable capacitive performance under repeated bending, folding, and compressing, which have been widely applied in flexible SCs. For example, Niu et al. [71] prepared a CNF/SWCNT hybrid non-woven macrofiber mat as the electrode material and charge collector, which was fabricated by simply controlling the extrusion patterns in an ethanol coagulation bath (Fig. 1). The SWCNTs are preferentially oriented along the axial direction of the macrofiber
which has a uniform diameter of \(\sim\,50\ \upmu\mathrm{m}\) and a good porous structure. Meanwhile, the CNF has effectively prevented the aggregation of SWCNTs due to its outstanding hydrophilicity. The resultant wearable SCs exhibited excellent electrochemical stability of \(96.0\%\) capacitance retention after 1,500 bending cycles and outstanding damage reliability of approximately \(93\%\) capacitance retention after a fifth extreme deformation.
Paper is by far the cheapest flexible substrate and typically includes a multiscale pore architecture defined by the cellulose fiber network. Papers with favorable properties (such as recyclability, mechanical flexibility, and biocompatibility) are good candidate substrates of the flexible energy storage applications, which have been used to support or absorb the relevant conducting materials. Paper-based flexible SCs can be prepared by printing, filtration, and coating methods, incorporating various carbonaceous materials (e.g., graphene and CNT), conductive polymers (e.g., PPy and PANI), and metal oxides (e.g., \(\mathrm{MnO}_{2}\) and \(\mathrm{V}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{5}\)) onto nanocellulose-based paper [74–77]. For instance, Kang et al. [78] made CNT/BNC papers electrode in which a CNT layer was deposited on the BC paper through a vacuum filtering progress relying on the hydrogen bonding and van der Waals interactions (Figs. 2(a)–2(d)). The SCs showed a specific capacitance of \(50.5\ \mathrm{F{\cdot}g^{-1}}\ \sim\ 20.2\ \mathrm{mF{\cdot}c m^{-2}}\) at a current density of \(1\ \mathrm{A{\cdot}g^{-1}}\). Moreover, the SCs maintained more than \(95\%\) capacitance after over 5,000 charge/discharge cycles, which exhibited excellent cyclic stability. Compared with other methods, the conformal coating can provide more mass loading and better mechanical properties, due to the active materials and cellulose paper strongly integrated in the structure. Wang et al. reported that the nanocellulose fibers are surface modified by introducing quaternary amine groups (c-NCFs), and then the pyrrole is conformally coated on the c-NCFs substrate through in situ polymerization (PPy@c-NCFs) [77]. The resultant electrodes showed both high normalized gravimetric \((127\ \mathrm{F{\cdot}g^{-1}})\) and volumetric \((122\ \mathrm{F{\cdot}c m^{-2}})\) capacitances at a super high current density of \(300\ \mathrm{mA{\cdot}c m^{-2}}\) \((\approx\,33\ \mathrm{A{\cdot}g^{-1}})\), superior to most paper-based supercapacitors. Besides, many researches have also proposed nanocellulose-based membranes with a high porosity used as electrolyte separators, which can provide excellent electrolyte retention and high ionic conductivity. For example, Zhang et al. [79] prepared a mesoporous cellulose membrane (m-Cel membrane) with uniform mesopores \((\approx\ 24.7\ \mathrm{nm})\) and high porosity \((71.78\%)\) through a solution-phase inversion process
(Fig. 2(e)). The m-Cel membrane exhibited high flexibility, good mechanical strength, high electrolyte retention of \(451.2\ \mathrm{wt.\%}\), and high ionic conductivity \((0.325\ \mathrm{S{\cdot}c m^{-1}})\) when saturated with KOH. Moreover, the electrode materials can be directly deposited on the m-Cel membrane-based polymer electrolyte (Fig. 2(f)). The as-prepared integrated planar-type micro-supercapacitor (MSC) exhibited high area capacitance \((153.34\ \mathrm{mF{\cdot}c m^{-2}})\) and volumetric capacitance \((191.66\ \mathrm{F{\cdot}c m^{-3}})\).
3D nanocellulose aerogels are flexible and highly porous materials, which exhibit unusual properties such as large specific surface area, high porosity, low density, and controllable doping structure, making them excellent substrates for energy devices. For example, Yang et al. [80] designed a lightweight chemically cross-linked CNC aerogel as a universal substrate, which was blended with PPy, PPy-CNT, and \(\mathrm{MnO}_{2}\). The hybrid aerogels showed good mechanical performance, which could remain intact when compressed in both air and aqueous electrolyte. Meanwhile, the hybrid aerogels assembled SC displayed excellent capacitance retention at high charge–discharge rates due to the high mass ratio of active material, as well as multiple channels for electronic and ionic diffusion. Wang et al. [13] developed a 3D lightweight CNF/PANI supermolecular aerogels (SA) with mechanical flexibility, shape recovery capabilities, hierarchically porous 3D structures (porosity of \(96.90\%\)), and high conductivity \((0.372\ \mathrm{mS{\cdot}c m^{-1}})\) (Fig. 2(g)). The SA was further fabricated into flexible SCs by combining SA with nonwoven polypropylene (PP) and \(\mathrm{PVA/H_{2}SO_{4}}\), which exhibited a high capacitance of \(291.01\ \mathrm{F{\cdot}g^{-1}}\) without the use of adhesive. Moreover, the SA has been used as a universal sensitive sensor for monitoring different gas concentration changes, human motion signals, and pulse beat signals.
Nanocellulose materials can also be transformed into carbon materials by simple carbonization. High temperature and hydrothermal carbonization are the most common way to build conductive carbon structures. The nanocellulose-derived carbon materials showed excellent electrical conductivity, large specific surface areas, and rich pore structures, which can be directly used as electrode materials in SCs. And, synthesis strategies such as activation treatment, doping with heteroatoms, and template methods have been extensively used to obtain higher electrochemical performance. Shan et al. [16] designed 3D honeycomb-like hierarchical structures carbon (HSC) via one-step carbonization/KOH activation of BC. The as-prepared HSC with
high specific surface area \((1,533\ \mathrm{cm}^{2}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1})\) showed a high specific capacitance \((422\;\mathrm{F{\cdot}g^{-1}}\) at \(2\ \mathrm{mV{\cdot}s^{-1}})\) and excellent rate performance \((73.7\%\) capacitance retention at \(500\;\mathrm{\mV{\cdot}s^{-1}})\) in KOH aqueous electrolyte. Chen et al. [81] prepared 3D hierarchical porous carbon aerogels (CNFAs) by using \(\mathrm{NaH}_{2}\mathrm{PO}_{4}\) to engineer the pyrolysis chemistry of CNF (Fig. 2(h)). The obtained CNFAs greatly improved the carbon yield and suppressed the volume shrinkage. Benefiting from the large specific surface area of \(514.70\,\mathrm{\m^{2}{\cdot}g^{-1}}\) as well as abundant nanochannels promoted the diffusion and transportation of electrolyte ions. The CNFAs- \(8\%\) achieved a superb capacitance of \(440.29\;\mathrm{F{\cdot}g^{-1}}\) at \(1\;\mathrm{A{\cdot}g^{-1}}\) , while the further assembled symmetric SCs showed outstanding rate capability \((63.29\%\) at \(10\ \mathrm{mA{\cdot}c m^{-2}})\) and superior cycling stability \((\sim100\%\) capacitance retention after 7,000 cycles). Most recently, Wang et al. [82] for the first time reported the preparation of
intrinsically conductive cellulose nanofiber derived from the ginger fiber at low temperature and pressure (Fig. 2(i)). The outer layer molecular chains of CNF were effectively converted into highly graphitized carbon and self-assembled into graphene layers on the original surface. The resultant DC-CNFene-4 exhibited high conductivity of \(1.099\ \mathrm{S{\cdot}c m^{-1}}\) and a specific capacitance of \(235.8\ \mathrm{F{\cdot}g^{-1}}\) . It is worth mentioning that the generality of this synthetic method has been verified from various biomass including wheat straw, bamboo pulp fibers, flax fiber, and aspen wood.
## 3.1.2 LIBs
LIBs have been employed as the main power source for various electronic devices, due to the advantages of high operation voltage,
high energy density, long lifespan, and environmental benignity [83, 84]. The charge/discharge mechanism of LIBs is achieved by the reversible movement of \(\mathrm{Li^{+}}\) ions between the cathode and anode material, and the performance is highly dependent on the active materials employed in the electrodes. For the cathode, lithium metal oxides with high positive redox potentials including \(\mathrm{LiCo}_{1-x}\mathrm{M}_{x}\mathrm{O}_{2}\), \(\mathrm{LiMn}_{1-x}\mathrm{M}_{x}\mathrm{O}_{2}\), \(\mathrm{LiMn}_{2-x}\mathrm{M}_{x}\mathrm{O}_{4}\), and \(\mathrm{LiMPO}_{4}\) (\(\mathbf{M}=\mathbf{F}\mathbf{e}\), Co, Ni, etc.) have most widely employed as positive electrode intercalation materials. Carbonaceous materials with long lifespan and low cost are the most used anode materials, but their theoretical storage capacity (\(<372\ \mathrm{mAh{\cdot}g^{-1}}\)), small practical energy density, and safety are the major concerns for large-scale energy storage applications. Benefiting from the intrinsic physicochemical properties and the advantages of its nanostructures, nanocellulose as a building block source can be developed as electrodes, separators, and electrolytes in LIBs. In the following sub-sections, we mainly focus our discussion on the application of nanocellulose-based materials in LIBs.
## 3.1.2.1 Electrodes
The conventional LIBs electrodes consist of active materials, conductive agents (e.g., carbon black), polymeric binders (e.g., polyvinylidene fluoride (PVDF)), and metal foil current collectors. Nanocellulose-based papers/films with high porosity and good mechanical strength can be used as a substrate for LIBs electrodes. Hu et al. [85] reported the Si-conductive nanopaper as LIBs anodes, where the Si was coated on conductive CNT-CNF nanopaper by plasma-enhanced chemical vapor deposition (PECVD) method (Figs. 3(a)–3(d)). The porous structure formed by CNF promoted the rapid transport of electrolyte ions. As a result, the Si-conductive nanopaper showed high stability that remained \(77\%\) capacity retention \((1,200\ \mathrm{mAh{\cdot}g^{-1}})\) after 100 deep charge/discharge cycles. Nanocellulose-based films can also be used to replace metallic current collectors in LIBs electrodes. Zhou et al. [86] fabricated a lamellar graphene/nanocellulose/silicon (GN/NC/Si) film as anode for flexible LIBs via a vacuum-assisted filtration method, where the NC as adhesive to crosslink GN and
Si (Figs. 3(e)–3(g)). Each GN layer can be regarded as a current collector, which highly improved the utilization of active materials. The prepared GN/NC/Si anode achieved an excellent reversible capacity of \(1,251\;\mathrm{mAh{\cdot}g^{-1}}\) after 100 cycles.
Nanocellulose-derived carbon materials with 3D porous networks can be directly employed as anode for LIBs. Wang et al. [87] prepared the BC-derived carbon aerogels by KOH activation of the pyrolyzed BC. The carbon aerogels as anode exhibited an excellent specific capacity of over \(857.6\;\mathrm{mAh{\cdot}g^{-1}}\) after 100 cycles at \(100\;\mathrm{mA{\cdot}g^{-1}}\) and maintained a high capacity of \(328\;\mathrm{mAh{\cdot}g^{-1}}\) even cycled at \(4,000\;\mathrm{mA{\cdot}g^{-1}}\). The nanocellulose-derived carbon materials can also be employed as conductive porous scaffolds to combine with metallic oxide or sulphate particles through an in situ growth method for the preparation of LIBs anode. Wang et al. [88] reported an anode with a unique 3D porous electrode structure, which was prepared by in situ assembly \(\mathrm{SnO}_{2}\) and/or Cu nanoparticles on BC-derived carbon aerogels (Fig. 3(h)). The \(\mathrm{PBC-SnO}_{2}\) hybrid aerogels showed a reversible capacity of ca. \(600\;\mathrm{mAh{\cdot}g^{-1}}\) after 100 cycles at \(100\;\mathrm{mA{\cdot}g^{-1}}\) and superior rate capacity. The specific capacity based on the total electrode weight is ca. \(380\;\mathrm{mAh{\cdot}g^{-1}}\) at \(1\;\mathrm{A{\cdot}g^{-1}}\), which is still higher than the theoretical capacity of graphite. Other active materials such as amorphous \(\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}\) [89], \(\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{4}\) nanoparticles [90], and \(\mathrm{MoS}_{2}\) nanoleaves [91] are also successfully synthesized on nanocellulose-derived carbon materials. Wan et al. [92] designed a novel 3D nano-\(\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}\)-decorated BC-based carbon aerogel \(\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}\)-BC-CNFs by a hydrothermal approach followed by carbonization (Figs. 3(i)–3(k)). The as-prepared \(\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}\)-BC-CNFs electrode exhibited a high reversible capacity of \(754\;\mathrm{mAh{\cdot}g^{-1}}\) after 100 cycles at \(100\;\mathrm{mA{\cdot}g^{-1}}\). The excellent electrochemical performance was attributed to the 3D porous structure and large surface area, which facilitated the diffusion of Li ions and accommodated volume changes of active materials.
Besides, NC can be used as the binder in LIBs electrode to replace the polymeric binder such as PVDF which needs the use of volatile and toxic organic solvents. Leijonmarck et al. [93] fabricated flexible positive electrodes by utilizing NFC as the binder. The electrodes exhibited good mechanical properties in both dry and soaked with electrolytes. Cao et al. [94] designed a free-standing \(\mathrm{Li}_{4}\mathrm{Ti}_{5}\mathrm{O}_{12}/\mathrm{CNT}/\mathrm{CNF}\) hybrid film with dual-layer structure as LIBs electrode, which was assembled by pressure-controlled aqueous extrusion progress (Fig. 3(l)). The CNF served as both building skeleton and binder in the hybrid film. Meanwhile, the CNT/CNF layer was also used as current collector in place of metallic foils. The flexible electrode showed high rate cycling performance and specific charge/discharge capacity of \(142\;\mathrm{mAh{\cdot}g^{-1}}\) even at a current rate of \(10\;\mathrm{C}\).
## 3.1.2.2 Separators
In LIBs, the separator plays a pivotal role in preventing the direct contact between the anode and cathode electrode, while providing ion-transport channels for the transportation of \(\mathrm{Li^{+}}\) ions. Polyethylene (PE) and PP are commonly used as separators in LIBs due to their high mechanical strength, good chemical stability, and suitable pore size [95]. However, the intrinsically hydrophobic, weak mechanical, poor electrolyte wettability, and low thermal stability greatly hinder their practical use in LIBs [96]. Recently, nanocellulose-based paper/film has gained great attention as a separator for LIBs, which was proven efficient in suppressing Li dendrite formation due to its high mechanical/thermal stability, excellent electrolyte uptake ability, and good Li wettability [97,98]. Chun et al. [96] firstly reported a CNF paper-derived (CNF) separator used in LIBs. The electrolytephilic, nanoporous structure was established between closely piled CNFs. Isopropyl alcohol was introduced as CNF-disassembling
agents, facilitating the evolution of highly porous structure by the loose packing of CNFs (Fig. 4(a)). The CNP separator exhibited good electrolyte wettability and low thermal shrinkage at \(150\;^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(0.5\;\mathrm{h}\) (Figs. 4(b) and 4(c)). \(\mathrm{SiO}_{2}\) nanoparticles can also be introduced as a CNF-disassembling agent to obtain a more porous structure [99]. The porous structure of separator can be fine-tuned by varying the \(\mathrm{SiO}_{2}\) content in the CNF suspension. The separator with \(5\%\;\mathrm{SiO}_{2}\) content showed the highest ionic conduction owing to the nanoporous structure and proper separator thickness. Pan et al. [100] designed a sandwich-structured separator composed of two surface layers of CNF and an intermediate layer of glass microfiber (GMF). The homogeneous distribution of nanopores in the CNF layer favors a homogeneous current distribution at both electrodes, and macropores at the intermediate GMF/CNF layer promote the ionic transport through the separator. The separator with pores structure showed a higher ionic conductivity than that for the commercial separator (1.14 vs. \(0.49\;\mathrm{mS{\cdot}cm^{-1}}\)), as well as the improvement of capacity and stability of lithium metal batteries.
Nanocellulose can also be simultaneously employed as separator and electrode materials in LIBs. Leijonmarck et al. [101] reported single-paper flexible LIBs, where NFC was used as both separator and electrode binder material. The battery paper was prepared through a paper-making type process of sequential filtration aqueous negative, separator, and positive electrode suspensions (Fig. 4(d)). The cross-sectional scanning electron microscopy (SEM) image showed three discrete and well-adhering layers, which were assigned to the positive, separator, and negative electrode (Figs. 4(e) and 4(f)). Wang et al. [102] designed all-NC-based paper LIBs, where CNF acts as separator and electrodes substrate materials as well as combined with carbon nanofibers formed 3D porous conducting cellulose paper (CCP) as current collectors (Fig. 4(g)). The porous structure of CCP yields a dendrite-free deposition of Li, which promoted the mass transport within the electrode and compensated for the volume changes during the cycling. And CNF separator with homogeneous pore distribution provided uniform current distributions at the electrode. With these advantages, the all-NC-based paper LIBs showed excellent cycling stability of \(91\%\) capacity retention after 800 cycles at a rate of \(2\;\mathrm{C}\).
## 3.1.2.3 Electrolytes
In currently commercial LIBs, liquid electrolytes such as carbonate-based organic solvents are mainly used as electrolytes due to high ionic conductivity. However, their volatility and flammability are prone to cause safety problems such as electrolyte leakage and battery combustion/explosion. To address the safety issues, the alternatives such as gel- and solid-type electrolytes have been widely researched. But, there is still a limitation in the further application that is poor mechanical strength. And the improvement of mechanical strength will cause a decrease in ionic conductivity. For the satisfaction of both mechanical property and conductivity, nanocellulose as a good filler material has been successfully employed in electrolyte systems. Chiappone et al. [103] prepared a gel polymer electrolyte membrane by free radical photo-polymerization process, which consists of a methacrylic-based polymer matrix and mechanical filler CNF (Figs. 4(h) and 4(i)). The Young's modulus and tensile strength of electrolyte membranes have been improved by the addition of the CNF. Even after swelling the electrolyte solution, the membranes still showed good mechanical behavior and dimensional stability. The electrolyte membranes also obtained a high ionic conductivity of approximately \(10^{-3}\;\mathrm{S{\cdot}cm^{-1}}\) at \(25\;^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\). The employment of CNF can also provide a thermal stabilization effect for the polymer electrolyte above the melting point of the polymer matrix [104].
The two-phase electrolytes were reported by Willgert et al. [105], which are composed of ionic conductive poly(ethylene glycol) (PEG) matrix along with the stiff CNF paper as reinforcement (Fig. 4(j)). The CNF was modified to obtain covalent bonds between the PEG and cellulose phase, to enhance the ionic conductivity after the composite was swelled with liquid electrolyte. A great elastic modulus \(>100\,\mathrm{MPa}\) (above \(100\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\)) and an ion conductivity of \(5\times10^{-5}\,\mathrm{S\cdot cm^{-1}}\) (at \(25\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\)) were obtained.
Compared with gel polymer electrolytes that still have liquid electrolytes characteristics, solid polymer electrolytes show higher safety. Poly(oxyethylene) (POE) based polymer electrolytes are the most commonly studied due to their strong solvation ability. Nevertheless, the high crystallinity of POE makes it need to be above the melting temperature \((\sim\,60\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C})\) to obtain proper conductivity, whereas the corresponding mechanical properties are very poor. Therefore, the design criterion for POE-based solid polymer electrolytes is generally the suppression of crystallization while ensuring mechanical properties. Nanocellulose-based fillers are proven to lower the crystallinity of the POE to promote ion migration and enhance mechanical properties [106, 107]. Samir et al. [108] reported nanocomposite polymer electrolytes consisting of a cross-linked polyether and lithium imide (LiTFSI) reinforced with CNC. The mechanical properties and thermal stability of nanocomposite electrolytes were significantly improved in the
presence of CNC, which is provided by the mechanical percolation mechanism of the CNC particles linked by hydrogen bonds. The ionic conduction of CNC filled electrolytes was also higher than unfilled electrolytes over the whole temperature range, especially in high temperature.
## 3.2 Solar cells
Solar energy as a renewable energy source has been extensively explored, due to the pursuit of sustainable development and nonpolluting energy. Substrates play a key role as the foundation for solar cells, since the critical properties such as mechanical strength, optical transparency, and maximum processing temperature determine its eligibility for its practical applications. Synthetic polymers such as polyethylene terephthalate (PET), polyethylene naphthalate (PEN), and polyethersulfone (PES) are the most commonly used substrates for solar cells. However, from a lifecycle perspective, these petroleum-based substrates are more expensive and environmentally unfriendly than easily recyclable or biodegradable substrates. Nanocellulose as an ideal substrate has been explored for use in solar cells, because its size is smaller than the wavelength of visible light, which makes the prepared nanocellulose-based paper with high transparency, as well as significant scattering along the light transport direction. Zhou et al. [109] reported polymer solar cells fabricated on optically
transparent CNC substrates, which exhibited excellent rectification in the dark and high power conversion efficiency (PEC) of \(2.7\%\) (Figs. 5(a) and 5(b)). The as-prepared solar cell can also be easily separated and recycled into individual components by a low-energy process at room temperature. Nevertheless, the low transmittance of the Ag bottom electrode limited the improvement of PEC. Zhou et al. [110] further prepared organic solar cells on recyclable CNC substrates wherein the polyethylenimine-modified Ag was used as the bottom electrode. The as-prepared solar cells displayed a maximum PEC of \(4.0\%\), which was comparable to that of reference solar cells on polyethersulfone substrates. Gao et al. [111] reported flexible perovskite solar cells (PSCs) based on the transparent nanocellulose paper (NCP) coated with acrylic resin, where the nanocellulose paper was prepared by using a solution containing \(\mathrm{NaOH/urea/H_{2}O}\) to dissolve cotton at low temperature. The resultant NCP-based PSCs exhibited a PCE of \(4.25\%\) and the
power per weight reached \(0.56\,\mathrm{~W\cdot g^{-1}}\). The flexible PSCs also showed good stability that maintained \(>80\%\) of its original PCE after 50 bending cycles. The performance of solar cells can be further improved by the enhancement of optical transparency and light scattering or haze. Fan et al. [112] demonstrated a novel transparent paper that was made from TEMPO-oxidized wood fibers (Fig. 5(c)). The high fragment content and fewer hollow structures of paper lead to a higher packing density, thus obtaining excellent optical transparency of \(\sim\,96\%\) and ultrahigh haze of \(\sim\,60\%\), as well as good mechanical strength compared to regular paper (Fig. 5(d)). Besides, the PCE of solar cells was efficiently increased from \(5.34\%\) to \(5.88\%\) by laminating a piece of transparent paper on indium tin oxide (ITO) glass (Fig. 5(e)), due to the better light harvesting from the diffused light. Later, Cao et al. [113] firstly reported the hole transport material (HTM)-free flexible PSCs fabricated on cellulose paper substrate (Fig. 5(f)). The as-prepared flexible PSCs showed an excellent PCE of \(9.05\%\)
and excellent stability against bending deformation which retained over \(75\%\) of its initial PCE after 1,000 bending cycles (Figs. 5(g) and 5(h)). The PSCs can also be successfully worn on a human wrist, proving the feasibility of application in wearable electronics.
Nanocellulose with network structure can be used as a template to prepare the mesoporous structure of functional material. Ghadiri et al. [114] reported nanostructured \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) hollow fibers with high porosity were prepared using CNF as a template (Figs. 5(i) and 5(j)). The \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) was coated on CNF, followed by \(500~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) calcination to remove the CNF template, forming a crosslinked \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) network which has remarkably enhanced electron transport properties than mesoporous films assembled from \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) nanoparticles. The CNF-templated \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) fibers were directly deposited on a transparent conducting glass substrate to serve as the photoanodes for dye-sensitized solar cells, which achieved absorbed photon-to-electrical current conversion yield exceeding \(95\%\) and a high PCE of \(7.2\%\). Nanocellulose can also be directly used as an electrode component in solar cells. Notte et al. [115] demonstrated a foldable cellulose/graphene nanocomposites electrode through spray coating. The flexible polymer solar cells based on nanocomposites electrode were assembled in an inverted configuration, because inverted structure can provide better compatibility with polymer (Fig. 5(k)). The as-prepared solar cells exhibited an over \(3\%\) PCE and the PCE was well preserved after 100 bending cycles with a radius of \(7~\mathrm{mm}\) (Fig. 5(l)). Ma et al. [116] prepared a transparent conductive AgNW-regenerated cellulose film as anode for perovskite solar cells. The conflict between optical transparency and conductivity caused by the introduction of AgNW was solved by \(\mathrm{HNO}_{3}\) treatment. The film exhibited a sheet resistance of \(29\ \Omega^{-1}\) and a transmittance of \(80\%\) at the wavelength of \(550~\mathrm{nm}\), while the resultant perovskite solar cells showed a PCE of \(4.49\%\). Additionally, nanocellulose can be modified as mechanical reinforcement materials in solar cells. Yuwawech et al. [117] reported the ethylene vinyl acetate (EVA) copolymer based solar cell encapsulating films which were modified by using esterified BC nanofibers. BC was chemically modified with propionic anhydride before compounding with EVA. And the introduction of esterified BC nanofibers improved the thermal and mechanical properties as it can delay the deacetylation of EVA. Meanwhile, the visible light transparency of the EVA films was retained over \(75\%\), due to the less entanglement and aggregation of the BC nanofibers after chemical modification.
## 3.3 TENGs
TENGs are an emerging mechanical energy harvesting technology that can convert environment mechanical energy (e.g., human movement, water wave, and vibration) into electrical energy through coupled triboelectric effect and electrostatic induction, which have unique merits including high power density, high efficiency, structural diversity, low cost, lightweight, and simple fabrication [118–120]. The TENGs are usually composed of two triboelectric layers with different charge polarities and conductive electrodes, and the most commonly used materials in the triboelectric layer are metals [121], polyamide (PA) [122], polydimethylsiloxane (PDMS) [123], PVDF [124], polytetrafluoroethylene (PTFE) [125], etc. Despite the excellent performances are presented by the traditional materials, the drawbacks such as environmental unfriendliness, poor biocompatibility, non-renewability, non-degradability, and high price still need to be solved. Nanocellulose has drawn great interest as a promising alternative, due to its abundance, biocompatibility, biodegradability, and triboelectric effect [126]. And the triboelectric sequence of nanocellulose is higher than most common polymers [127], which makes it an ideal positive material
for TENGs. Yao et al. [120] first prepared the nanocellulose-based TENG in 2006 by pairing the TEMPO-oxidized CNF thin films and fluorinated ethylene propylene (FEP). The assembled TENG devices with good transparency showed a maximum open circuit voltage and short circuit current of \(32.8~\mathrm{V}\) and \(35~\mathrm{mA}\) under the active layer area of \(40~\mathrm{cm}^{2}\), which were comparable to the TENG devices composed of typical synthetic polymers. Moreover, the TENG was integrated with a fiberboard made from recycled cardboard, which could light up 35 green LEDs with electricity generated by one single step on the fiberboard, demonstrating the feasibility of practical application. Kim et al. [128] reported the assembly of regenerated BC film with Cu foil to form a vertical contact–separation mode Bio-TENG, where the uniform BC film with transparent and flexible was obtained by dissolving and regenerating purified BC through a nitration method (Figs. 6(a) and 6(b)). The \(5\ \mathrm{cm}\times5\ \mathrm{cm}\) Bio-TENG displayed an open circuit voltage of \(13~\mathrm{V}\) under an external force condition of \(16.8~\mathrm{N}\) and 1 \(\mathrm{Hz}\), while the accumulative charge and peak-power density were \(8.1\,\upmu\mathrm{C}\cdot\mathrm{m}^{-2}\) and \(4.8\,\mathrm{mW}\cdot\mathrm{m}^{-2}\) at a load resistance of \(1\,\mathrm{M}\Omega\).
The output performance of nanocellulose-based TENGs can be improved by increasing the contact area of friction layers. For example, Zhang et al. [129] developed a polymer porous aerogel film-based TENGs with an active layer area of \(2\;\mathrm{cm}^{2}\), consisting of highly porous CNF (P-CNF) as tribopositive layer and porous PDMS (P-PDMS) as tribonegative layer. The P-CNF aerogel film with an interconnected nanofiber-like network was prepared by freeze-drying and compression steps, which exhibited a Brunauer–Emmett–Teller (BET) surface area of \(68\;\mathrm{m}^{2}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) and high porosity of \(84\%\). For comparison, the dense CNF film (D-CNF) was prepared by a film casting method. The P-CNF/P-PDMS TENG generated an output voltage of \(22.3\,\mathrm{~V~}\) and current of \(2.2\,\upmu\mathrm{A}\) under an external force condition of \(6\,\mathrm{N}\) and \(10\,\mathrm{Hz}\), as well as a power density of \(0.25\,\mathrm{~W}\cdot\mathrm{m}^{-2}\) which was over 8-fold higher than D-CNF film-based TENG \((0.03\ \mathrm{V}\cdot\mathrm{m}^{-2})\), due to the increase in contact area and electrostatic induction in the porous structure. Meanwhile, the P-CNF/P-PDMS TENG also showed excellent cyclic stability, the output voltage signals with no obvious change after 10,000 cycles. Qian et al. [130] prepared an all-printed TENG through a direct-write printing approach, where TEMPO-oxidized CNF ink and PDMS ink were printed onto the \(\mathrm{Ag/PET}\) substrates forming tribopositive layer and tribonegative layer (Fig. 6(c)). The printed CNF/Ag/PET layer was further processed by freeze-drying to improve surface roughness. The obtained CNF aerogel/Ag/PET layer with 3D micro/nano hierarchically patterned structure and the contact area was almost 1.75 times larger than the flat microporous TENG fabricated by a traditional solution mold casting method. As a consequence, the output performance of all-printed TENG was better than flat microporous TENG, and the all-printed TENG with a tilt angle of \(0^{\circ}\) exhibited a high output voltage of \(55.8~\mathrm{V}\) and current of \(0.94~\upmu\mathrm{A}\) under an external force condition of \(4\,\mathrm{N}\) and \(4\,\mathrm{Hz}\).
The output performance of nanocellulose-based TENGs can also be improved by the addition of conductive materials. For example, He et al. [131] reported a cellulose fiber based TENG (cf-TENG), where CMFs/CNFs/Ag membrane with hierarchical 2D nanostructure was prepared by adding Ag nanofibers to CNFs/CMFs paper. The cf-TENG composed of CMFs/CNFs/Ag membrane as the positive electrode and FEP film as the negative electrode showed excellent mechanical stability and flexibility that can be bent over \(90~^{\circ}\!C\). The cf-TENG generated an open circuit voltage of \(21.9~\mathrm{V}\), short-circuit current of \(0.73~\upmu\mathrm{A}\), and maximum output power of \(7.68\,\mathrm{\mW{\cdot}c m}^{-2}\) at a load resistance of \(20\,\mathrm{\,M}\Omega\). Besides, the cf-TENG system exhibited a high PM 2.5 removal efficiency of \(98.83\%\) and can also be used as a self-power sensor for breath monitoring. Dielectric and ferroelectric materials have
also been added to nanocellulose to prepare high-performance TENGs [132]. Shao et al. [133] prepared a \(\mathrm{BC/BaTiO}_{3}\) composites film that the \(\mathrm{BaTiO}_{3}\) particles with high dielectric constant were introduced into BC film by a vacuum filtration process. The addition of \(\mathrm{BaTiO}_{3}\) particles improved the surface roughness of \(\mathrm{BC/BaTiO}_{3}\) composites film, which is over 3 times than that of pure BC film. Combined with the improvement of dielectric constant and contact area, the \(\mathrm{BC/BaTiO}_{3}\)-PDMS TENG generated an open circuit voltage of \(181\ \mathrm{~V~}\) and short-circuit current of \(21~\mathrm{mA}\) under an external force condition of \(42~\mathrm{N}\) and \(2\:\mathrm{Hz}\), which were 1.5 and 2.2 times higher than those of pure BC-PDMS TENG. Meanwhile, the \(\mathrm{BC/BaTiO}_{3}\)-PDMS TENG achieved a maximum output power of \(4.8\,\mathrm{\mW{\cdot}c m^{-2}}\) at a load resistance of \(40\,\mathrm{M}\Omega\). Similarly, nanocellulose can be used as fillers to improve TENGs performance. Peng et al. [134] used CNC as effective dielectric fillers to prepare PDMS/CNC composite film by spin coating (Fig. 6(d)). The assembled \(1.5\:\mathrm{cm}\times1.5\:\mathrm{cm}\) TENG based on Al positive electrode and PDMS/CNC film negative electrode showed a high open circuit voltage of \(320~\mathrm{V}\) and short-circuit current of \(11.25~\upmu\mathrm{A}\) under an external force condition of \(40\,\mathrm{N}\) and \(10\,\mathrm{Hz}\). These results were 2 to 4 times higher than those of the pure PDMS TENGs under the same conditions, due to the presence of positively charged CNC can produce an external electric field to facilitate the transfer of more electrons to the PDMS surface. Moreover, the PDMS/CNC TENG was able to light up 100 LEDs in the compressive force of \(40\,\mathrm{N}\).
## 3.4 MEEGs
MEEGs have shown great potential for green sustainable energy harvesting, which can convert atmospheric moisture energy into electricity without the restrictions of environmental and regional [135, 136]. In MEEGs, the electricity is generated by the interactions of moisture and nanomaterial, where water molecules in the atmosphere are absorbed by hydrophilic materials with functional groups (e.g., \(-\mathrm{OH}\), \(-\mathrm{COOH}\), and \(-\mathrm{SO}_{3}\mathrm{H}\)), leading to the release and migration of anions or cations [137, 138]. And the
mechanisms of MEEGs can be mainly divided into ions gradient diffusion and streaming current according to the source of migration charges. Obviously, MEEGs are in their infancy compared with other energy conversion devices and currently have drawbacks such as low power density and low output voltage \((<\,1.5\,\mathrm{~V~})\) [139]. However, the development of MEEGs is still expected to improve the global environmental problems and energy crisis, due to water is the largest energy carrier on earth. Nanocellulose has favorable advantages including abundant functional groups, large specific surface area, hydrophilicity, and porous structure, which is considered as the ideal material to improve the electrical output of MEEGs. Yang et al. [140] prepared an asymmetric ionic aerogel consisting of TEMPO-CNFs (top) and quatern-CNFs (bottom) through a facile freeze-casting method. This aerogel showed a hierarchical porous structure composed of oriented microscale pores and nanoscale pores, which is beneficial for the capturing of water from the air and forming ion-conducting networks with hydrated nanochannels. Meanwhile, the high porosity of \(99.2\%{-}99.6\%\) can be obtained by adjusting the CNFs concentrations. The further assembled MEEG consisted of the asymmetric ionic aerogel sandwiched between two Pt electrodes, which showed a maximum voltage output of \(115\,\mathrm{\mV}\) at \(90\%\) relative humidity (RH). Li et al. [141] reported a wood-derived ionic aerogel as hygroscopic materials for the MEEG (Fig. 6(e)), the continuous cellulose nanonetworks like spiderwebs were formed around the inner walls of aerogel microchannels under the modulation of LiCl. The MEEG was further fabricated by an ionic aerogel and two electrodes (open electrode Cu grid and enclosed electrode Ag foil). Attributed to sufficient mobile ions \(\left(\mathrm{Li}^{+}\right)\)and continuous ionic bridges, the single MEEG achieved a high voltage of \(750\;\mathrm{mV}\) and surprising current output of \(715\;\mathrm{mV}\) at \(60\%\) relative humidity, as well as excellent stability of keeping the voltage without obvious change for \(10\,\mathrm{h}\) when the humidity suddenly dropped below \(10\%\). Besides, six MEEG connected in series generated a voltage up to \(140\,\mathrm{V}\), which can light up the LED.
The electrical output of MEEGs can also be improved by surface treatments of nanocellulose [142]. For example, Lee et al. [143] prepared a graphitic carbon layer (GCL) directly onto the TEMPO-oxidized CNF substrate by infrared laser irradiation (Figs. 6(f) and 6(g)), and a vertical concentration gradient was achieved that the conversion of CNF to GCL on the top surface was higher than the bottom surface. Correspondingly, there is an oxygen-to-carbon ratio (O/C) gradient between the top and bottom surfaces, so the hydrolysis of the carboxyl groups in the GCL generated a hydrogen ion concentration gradient between the top and bottom surfaces, which can create electricity. The \(3\ \mathrm{mm}\times\,3\ \mathrm{mm}\) MEEG device was composed of Ag-paste-coated glass substrate, GCL, and \(\mathrm{Cu}\) mesh, which exhibited a voltage of \(830\;\mathrm{\mV}\) and current output of \(5.93\;\;\upmu\mathrm{A}{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}\) at \(82\%\) relative humidity, as well as three MEEGs connected in series was able to light up three LEDs.
## 3.5 Sensors
Sensor technologies have attracted great interest in the application of different fields such as the protection of human health, environmental monitoring, and industrial safety [144]. With the rapid growth of the Internet of Things, the world sensor market is projected to need more than one trillion units per year by 2022 [145]. However, the conventional metal and inorganic semiconductor-based sensors are limited by their drawbacks such as strain detection range and complex fabrication progress, as well as their rigidity caused the incompatible with flexible devices. It is therefore urgent to develop simple, cost-effective, and flexible materials for the application in the sensor. Nanocellulose-based materials with extraordinary electrical, optical, and mechanical properties as attractive alternatives have been employed in several sensing applications such as environmental monitoring, food safety, physical sensing, human disease detection, and healthcare. In the subsequent subsections, we will discuss in detail the sensing applications of nanocellulose-based materials.
## 3.5.1 Biosensors
Surface-enhanced Raman spectroscopy (SERS) is an analysis of the molecular vibration structure with a trace substance, which is considered as one of the most powerful tools for chemical and biological detection due to its high sensitivity and selectivity. The SERS signals of the analyte are amplified through the electromagnetic interaction of light with metal nanoparticles (e.g., silver and gold) known as localized surface plasmon resonances (LSPR). Nanocellulose with unique flexible and 3D interconnected hierarchical structure is considered as desirable substrates in SERS sensing to achieve high signal enhancement, as well as prevent the agglomeration of metal nanoparticles [146, 147]. For example, Kim et al. [148] reported a label-free biosensor, which was prepared by two-stage successive ionic layer absorption and reaction techniques (Fig. 7(a)). The raspberry-like bimetallic core@shell nanoparticles with the highly polycrystalline structure were well dispersed on cellulose paper, and a superior SERS enhancement factor \((3.98\,\times\,10^{8}\) SERS:EF) of cellulose strip was achieved. The bimetallic sensor showed high sensitivity and selectivity for determining prohibited drugs (e.g., aniline, sodium azide, and malachite green) in aquatic environments, while can monitor the progress of breast cancer through urine. Kim et al. [149, 150] also further developed the cellulose paper-based SERS sensors for the early detection of prenatal diseases and subarachnoid hemorrhage induced complications.
The level of blood components is highly associated with various diseases and tissue-organ damage in humans, thus the development of sensitive blood test technologies is of increasing importance. Wang et al. [151] designed a bienzymatic glucose biosensor that two enzymes, glucose oxidase, and horseradish peroxidase were immobilized on the surface of the Au-BC nanocomposite modified glassy carbon electrode while their biocatalytic activities were maintained. The biosensor was successfully used for the determination of glucose in human blood samples, which showed a fast response, satisfying stability, good
anti-interference, reproducibility, and linearity in the range from 10 to \(400\;\upmu\mathrm{M}\). Gao et al. [152] introduced a cellulose paper-based lateral flow strip (PLSF) that Au nanostar@Raman Reporter@silica sandwich nanoparticles as the SERS probes to obtained high sensitivity (Fig. 7(b)). The SERS-paper-based lateral flow strip (PLFS) showed a good limit of detection (LOD) and excellent ability of anti-interference in a blood plasma-containing sample matrix, which was 1 and 3 orders of magnitude lower than colorimetric PLFS, respectively. The SERS-PLFS was employed for detection of neuron-specific enolase (NSE), a traumatic brain injury (TBI) protein biomarker, and achieved a linear detection range from 1.0 to \(75.0~\mathrm{ng\cdot mL^{-1}}\) toward NSE in a mixture of \(80\%\) blood plasma and \(20\%\) PBS. Furthermore, the SERS-PLFS was also successfully employed for the detection of NSE levels in clinical blood plasma samples taken from deidentified TBI patients. Naghdi et al. [153] reported a nanopaper-based analytical device for visual sensing of human serum albumin (HSA) in human blood serums, which was based on curcumin embedded BC nanopaper (CEBC) (Fig. 7(c)). The color changes of CEBC (from red to orange) were monitored by visually (naked eye/smartphone camera) or spectrophotometer that as analytical signals for HAS, due to the HAS showed an inhibitory effect on the curcumin degradation in alkaline media (Fig. 7(d)). The as-prepared NAD/CEBC as an albumin assay kit was successfully employed in the detection of HAS in human blood serum samples. The result of NAD/CEBC was in good agreement with that obtained by the BCG Albumin assay kit as a clinical routine-method for HAS detection. Human tears include various biochemical molecules (e.g., proteins, metabolites, and organic acids), and the disease-associated biomarkers in the tears also provide opportunities for daily healthcare sensing and monitoring. Park et al. [154] developed a plasmonic Schirmer strip, which was used for quantitative SERS detection of uric acid in human tears (Figs. 7(e)–7(g)). The Au nanoislands were uniformly formed on CNF matrices surface by controlling the film thickness and the deposition rate. And the hygroscopic pores of CNF matrices were effectively retained, which is beneficial to collected human tears, as well as provided highly sensitive SERS detection of biomarkers in tears owing to the volumetric electromagnetic hot spots. A strong linear correlation between uric acid level in both human tears and blood for gouty arthritis diagnosis was proved, which demonstrated the potential of healthcare monitoring using human tears.
Point-of-care (POC) diagnostics is speedily developed over the decades, which shows significant potential in clinical diagnosis and health evaluation. Uddin et al. [155] reported a disposable microfluidic sensor based on CNF for glucose detection. The CNF was introduced for the preparation of microfluidic channels and immobilization of glucose oxidase. The enzyme activity retention was improved in the POC diagnostic unit that can detect the concentration of glucose \((<0.1\times10^{-3}\;\mathrm{M})\) from fluid matrices in less than \(10\ \mathrm{min}\), as well as the color change detection was simply done by using a mobile phone's camera. Tabatabaee et al. [156] demonstrated a photoluminescent nanopaper-based assay kit for the detection of jaundice-causing Bilirubin (BR) levels in infant blood (Fig. 7(h)). The carbon dot (CD) as sensing probe was embedded in the BC nanopaper substrate by a simple dropping method. The nanosensor successfully determined the BR through an integrated smartphone camera, and the obtained recovered photoluminescence (PL) intensity was linearly proportional to the concentration of BR in the range of \(2{-}20~\mathrm{mg{\cdot}dL^{-1}}\). Furthermore, the result of nanosensor was in good agreement with that obtained by a clinical reference method for the determination of BR concentration in infant blood, which exhibited the clinical efficiency and usability of the nanosensor.
## 3.5.2 Physical sensors
Artificial electronic skin (e-skin) is designed to mimic the mechanical and sensory properties of human skin, which can convert the external stimulus such as strain, pressure, temperature, and humidity into electric signals, resulting in great attention due to their potential applications in various areas including human–machine interface, robotic prosthetics, and health monitoring. Nanocellulose with unique properties can be easily made into 1D fibers [157, 158], 2D materials (e.g., films and membrane) [159, 160], and 3D materials (e.g., hydrogels and aerogels) [13, 161], which are attractive candidates for flexible e-skin sensors. For example, Zhu et al. [162] reported a CNCs enhanced silk fibroin (SF) membrane-based e-skin sensor, in which CNCs as cross-linked sites promoted the formation of a dynamic network of SF membrane through intermolecular hydrogen bonding interaction. The SF/CNCs membranes with \(15\;\mathrm{wt.\%}\) CNCs showed higher Young's modulus of \(960\;\mathrm{MPa}\) and great transparency of \(90\%\). The dynamic reversible networks between SF and molecular chain imparted the SF/CNCs membranes excellent multi-bend sensing ability that the bending response was better than pure SF membrane (Figs. 8(a) and 8(b)). Nanocellulose can also incorporate with conductive materials (e.g., CNT, PANI, and PPy) to improve both conductivity and mechanical strength [163, 164]. Song et al. [165] demonstrated self-healing hydrogels sensors consisting of polycarboxylic multibranched CNC and PANI (Fig. 8(c)). The hydrogels exhibited high breaking strength of \(171.52\ \mathrm{kPa}\) and ultra-stretchability of \(1885\%\), due to the dynamic hydrogen bonds interactions and hierarchical structure. The high self-healing efficiency was achieved in both air and underwater that can reach up to \(99.56\%\) within \(120\ \mathrm{s}\). The hydrogel sensor was capable of detecting and distinguishing various human motions such as swallow, wrist, fingers, elbow, tummy, and knee joints in real-time. Wang et al. [164] reported hybrid CNF/CNT aerogels, which were prepared by a freeze-drying method. With The combination of electrical properties of CNT and bulk availability of CNF, the hybrid aerogels showed good pressure sensing properties. Chen et al. [166] demonstrated compressible and elastic carbon aerogels (CECAs) consisting of MXene \(\left(\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{C}_{2}\right)\) nanosheets and BC. The BC fiber with a high aspect ratio and flexibility was easily entangled, which can be used as nanobinder to connected MXene nanosheets resulting in continuous and wave-shaped lamellae. The oriented alignment of lamellae imparted the CECAs excellent compressibility and elasticity. The CECAs exhibited super high strain of \(99\%\) over 100 cycles and long-term compression at \(50\%\) strain for at least 100,000 cycles, due to the excellent structural stability. The resultant CECAs sensor was successfully used for the detection of tiny pressure and strain signals in a wide working pressure range \((0{-}10\;\mathrm{kPa})\), as well as showed a high linear sensitivity in a broad strain range \((0\%{-}95\%)\). The CECAs sensor can also detect various human motions such as speaking, face expressions, jugular venous pulse, arm pulse, finger, elbow, and wrist bending, which showed great potential application in flexible wearable devices.
Temperature monitoring is highly needed in diverse heterogeneous environments such as industrial engineering, environmental regulation systems, and biomedical fields, where high-precision temperature information is significant for efficient and reliable operation. Nanocellulose-based flexible substrates have been successfully employed in the preparation of temperature sensors. For example, Jung et al. [167] reported a fully integrated CNF-based thermoelectric sensor for the detection of temperature. The CNF film was a suitable substrate for inkjet printing due to the capacity of absorption of conductive materials
without dissolving in water. The thermocouple was formed by printing PEDOT:PSS, Ag nanoparticles, and CNTs as thermoelectric materials on the CNF film with inkjet printing, while the temperature monitoring was achieved by detecting the temperature difference which is caused by Seebeck voltage (Figs. 8(d) and 8(e)). The obtained temperature sensor generated a thermoelectric voltage output of \(1.7\ \mathrm{mV}\) for a temperature difference of \(125\,\mathrm{~K~}\) with a sensitivity of \(11\ \upmu\mathrm{V}{\cdot}\mathrm{K}^{-1}\) . However, conventional physical temperature probes such as thermocouples and thermometers have limitations in noncontact and dynamic systems. Optical thermometric as an alternative has attracted great attention in the fabrication of temperature sensors. Zhang et al. [168] demonstrated a dual-emitting fluorescent phosphor with CNC-assisted carbon dots grafted SAO \(\mathrm{(SrAl_{2}O_{4},\ E u^{2+},}\) and \(\mathrm{Dy}^{2+}\) ) for temperature sensing. The surface characteristics of phosphors were further modified by coupling amino-silane and assembled with CNF to form the flexible isotropic film. The fluorescent emissions of films showed a linearity behavior with surrounding temperature variation (243–383 K).
Humidity sensors play a vital role in many applications including weather prediction, agriculture, industrial manufacturing, medical monitoring, and wearable electronics. Most commercially available humidity sensors are made from porous ceramics or metal oxides. However, the rigid structure and complicated integration process limit their applications, as well as the accuracy and sensitivity of traditional sensors are restricted by high humidity and high temperature. Therefore, the development of flexible humidity sensors with a wide response range, high sensitivity, and good stability is highly needed. Nanocellulose-based films with excellent flexibility and versatility are attractive candidates for the preparation of flexible humidity sensors. For instance, Wang et al. [169] reported a CNF-based humidity sensor, which was proven to induce humidity-controlled reversible actuation. The bending extent of CNF film relied on the humidity difference across the film and was affected by the film thickness. The obtained CNF-based humidity sensor showed high sensitivity responding to the low humidity of human hands from a distance of several millimeters. Nanocellulose can also be
combined with conductive materials to improve the sensitivity of humidity sensors. Xu et al. [170] fabricated poly(vinyl alcohol) (PVA)/CNF/rGO composites films for humidity sensing by a casting method. CNF with the inherent amphiphilic property was utilized to stabilize the dispersion of rGO sheets in composites films, leading to the improvement of conductivity and mechanical performance. Meanwhile, CNF also functioned as a bridge to constructed water molecules channels between PVA and rGO sheets to improve the sensitivity of humidity. The conductivity of composites films changed rapidly with the variations in RH and showed good repeatability between \(50\%-70\%\) RH. Zhu et al. [171] developed a flexible CNF/CNT composite film humidity sensor through fast vacuum filtration. TEMPO-oxidized CNF functioned as a dispersant to improve the dispersion of CNTs while used as sensitive materials to enhance the humidity performance of the composite film (Figs. 8(f) and 8(g)). The resultant CNF/CNTs-5wt. \(\%\) humidity sensor showed a high response between \(11\%-95\%\) RH, as well as the response value reached a maximum of \(69.9\%\) \(\left(\Delta I/I_{0}\right)\) at \(95\%\) RH (Figs. 8(h) and 8(i)). The sensor also exhibited excellent bending resistance and long-term stability, and it was also successfully employed in the monitoring of human breath.
## 3.5.3 Chemical sensors
Heavy metals ions detection plays a significant role in many areas such as environmental safety, food industry, and medicine, due to the absence or presence over the requisite amount may cause severe risks for the ecosystem and human health. Thus, the development of metal ion monitoring technologies with sensitivity, stability, and selectivity is in high demand. Zhang et al. [172] designed a pyrene-modified CNC fluorescent chemosensor for metal ions sensing, where the fluorescence emission of pyrene was improved after modified onto CNC. The resultant chemosensor showed excellent selectivity towards \(\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}\) among other metal ions, as well as good discrimination between \(\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}\) and \(\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}\). Weishaupt et al. [173] prepared a protein-CNF paper-based sensor for copper ions detection by combining the sensing biomolecule cyanobacterial C-phycocyanin (CPC) and TEMPO-oxidized CNF matrix. The detection of copper was by the utilization of the influence of copper concentration on the fluorescence emission of CPC. The CySense films can be analyzed by various techniques including fluorescence spectrometry and microarray laser scanning owing to the reproducible morphological and optical features. The obtained sensor showed a lower detection limit of \(200\times10^{-9}\,\mathrm{M}\) and an IC50 of \(4.9\times10^{-6}\,\mathrm{M}\) as changes in fluorescence emission intensity. Abbasi-Moayed et al. [174] developed a BC nanopaper-based ratiometric fluorescent sensor array (NRFCA) as an artificial tongue for chemical discrimination application, which was inspired by the mammalian olfactory and gustatory system. The carbon dots-rhodamine B (CD-RhB) was introduced on the BC nanopaper platform to enhance the visual discrimination analysis, due to the cross-reaction interaction between the CD-RhB nanohybrids and the heavy metal ions caused the color emission change of the sensing elements (Fig. 8(j)). The as-prepared sensor array was able to distinguish five heavy metal ions \(\left(\mathrm{Hg}^{2+},\,\mathrm{Pb}^{2+},\,\mathrm{Cd}^{2+},\,\mathrm{Fe}^{3+},\right.\) and \(\left.\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}\right)\) while successfully identifying the heavy metal ions in the water and fish samples.
Gas, as well as toxic gas monitoring, is also critical to environmental protection, industry, and public health. Dai et al. [175] proposed a colorimetric sensor for toxic ammonia \(\left(\mathrm{NH}_{3}\right)\) gas detecting, in which the copper(II)-doped cholesteric CNC film maintained the chiral nematic signature and optical activity of CNC. A red shift of reflective wavelength and an effective colorimetric transition were presented as the \(\mathrm{NH}_{3}\) merged into the nematic layers of CNC and trigger-sensed to copper ions chelated
on CNC. Koga et al. [145] prepared a paper-based molecular sensor for the detection of \(\mathrm{NO}_{2}\), wherein a CNF paper substrate, \(\mathrm{ZnO}\) nanowire sensor, and graphite electrodes were integrated by two-step papermaking and pencil-draining processes (Fig. 8(k)). The \(\mathrm{ZnO}\) nanowire/CNF composite networks were implanted on the CNF paper substrate surface, which provided efficient accessibility of target molecules to the nanowire and reliable electrical contact with electrodes. The obtained paper sensor showed efficient resistance change on the sensing of \(\mathrm{NO}_{2}\), as well as cut-and-paste usability and easy dispersability. Besides, the sensing of volatile organic compounds such as ethanol, toluene, and propane is also very important. Zhang et al. [176] demonstrated an electrochemical gas sensor for alcohol detecting, which was equipped with a cofunctionalized CNF/GO membrane. The CNF/GO membrane with a 3D interpenetrating network structure as solid-electrolyte showed excellent in-plane and through-plane proton conductivities and high water retention, due to the graft of sulfonic acid groups onto the surface of CNF and GO. The resultant sensor exhibited a great response to ethanol vapor at different concentrations with a low detection limit of approximately \(25\,\mathrm{ppm}\), as well as excellent linearity and sensitivity.
## 4 Conclusions and perspective
Nanocellulose as green sustainable materials has numerous advantages including excellent mechanical properties, large specific surface area, high aspect ratio, low density, and rich surface chemistry, making it an ideal substrate/component for energy and sensing devices. Benefiting from the unique structure and physicochemical properties, nanocellulose is easy to form functional materials with various nanostructures such as 1D fibers, 2D papers/films, and 3D aerogels/hydrogels. As discussed in this article, nanocellulose has been widely explored to combine with conductive materials such as carbon nanomaterials (CNTs, graphene, etc.), conductive polymer (PPy, PANI, etc.), metal/metal oxide nanoparticles (Ag, Au, \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\), \(\mathrm{ZnO}\), etc.), MOFs, and COFs to prepare conductive composites, where nanocellulose acts as a reinforcing agent, a dispersant, a supporter or a substrate, etc. in composite materials. Besides, nanocellulose is also an ideal carbon precursor which can convert into conductive carbonaceous materials through simple carbonization, and the use of synthesis strategies such as activation treatment and heteroatom doping is able to achieve higher electrochemical performance. Thus, nanocellulose has been extensively employed as a functional electrode element for SCs, LIBs, solar cells, and TENGs. With high thermal stability, wettability, and high porosity, nanocellulose also shows broad application prospects in electrolytes and separators for SCs and LIB, as well as the hygroscopic materials for MEEGs. In addition, nanocellulose with flexibility and excellent mechanical properties has been integrated into sensor systems, greatly enhancing the sensing performance and promoting the development of sensors.
Although nanocellulose-based functional materials have experienced a period of rapid development in energy-related devices, their large-scale practical applications still face many challenges. The longitudinal size and porous structure of nanocellulose are yet poorly controlled. Remarkably, the micromesopores structure and porosity of nanocellulose-based composites have a significant impact on electrochemical performance. Nanocellulose is an inherently insulating material, and how to engineer the interface structure between conductive materials and nanocellulose to reduce its influence on the conductivity of composites is also an urgent problem to be solved. How to integrate multi-functions into nanocellulose-based composites is also an important direction of future research. Besides, the technology of large-scale commercial production of
nanocellulose-based composites still needs to be explored, especially on the premise of being green and environmentally friendly.
In summary, the research on nanocellulose-based functional materials in the energy-related fields is just at the beginning. But in the background of the global environmental and energy crisis, research interest in green and sustainable nanocellulose will continue to increase, and more advanced technologies will emerge in the future.
